has_generated": null, "main_agent_searched": false, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": 1000000, "model_max_tokens": 128000, "output_tail": "Adding a regression test, then running it.\n`round_money(2.675)` now returns `2.68`. I ran the new tests and the existing `test_split` directly with `python3`, since pytest isn't installed here, and all of them pass. The old code still gives `round(2.675, 2) == 2.67` and `round(0.125, 2) == 0.12`.\n\n**Why it was wrong:** `round(x, 2)` was failing in two ways:\n1. A float can't hold 2.675 exactly. It's stored as `2.67499999\u2026`, so it rounds down.\n2. Python's `round()` sends exact halves to the even digit (banker's rounding), not up. That also breaks `0.125`, which gave `0.12`.\n\n**Fix** (`pricing/money.py`): `round_money` now rounds the number as it's written, using `Decimal(repr(x)).quantize(Decimal(\"0.01\"), rounding=ROUND_HALF_UP)`, and returns a float as before. `add_vat` gets the fix too, because it calls `round_money`.\n\nNegative halves now round away from zero, so `-2.675` becomes `-2.68`.\n\n**Regression test:** `tests/test_round_money.py` checks the stored-float cases (`2.675`, `1.005`), the even-digit cases (`0.125`, `-2.675`), and ordinary values. There's no git repo here, so I couldn't run the test against the old code. It does fail against plain `round()`, which gives `2.67` and `0.12`.", "paced_wait_s": 0.0, "permission_rejections": 0, "read_calls": 2, "replies_ok": 9, "replies_throttled": 0, "requests_cache_read": 253732, "requests_cache_write": 7783, "requests_cost_usd": 0.1360814, "requests_count": 9, "requests_input_uncached": 20, "requests_output": 2317, "reread": false, "rule_order": "none", "rule_prefix": "neither", "run_id": "omp:trig-omp-bugfix-regress:claude-opus-5-5:trial5", "run_seed": 20261014, "sampling_observed": null, "schedule_index": 9, "score": 1.0, "started_at": "2026-10-13T06:08:36.872010+00:00", "subagent_resumed": false, "subagent_write_calls": 0, "success": true, "t_